Original commit body from b6cdd1b457edefc200f69b5d84d49c1eade99312:

INV-bigaz's statement is "a change to a non-Python input the production
code loads at runtime must select the tests that exercise it". The fix
that landed in 9d552f3bd1 keyed on YAML, which is 169 of the 177
non-Python files under packages/*/src -- and the eight it missed are not
inert. url_folding/SCOPE.md is parsed by get_scoped_languages() on every
run that folds a URL, and it selected nothing: the same keyed-to-one-
instance shape one level down from the defect that commit closed.

The key is now "not Python" rather than "is YAML". Python is excluded
because the import slice already resolves it, which is also what makes
the widening safe -- a linker module two levels below src/ is the real
risk and is the case the tests name.

The shell fallback keeps its own copy of the predicate, because it has to
work when python3 or the helper is missing and silence is the worst
failure mode for a selector. Two homes for one fact is how a widening
lands in one and not the other, so a test walks one representative of
every path shape in the tree and requires the two to agree. Both homes
were mutation-tested: removing either exclusion turns two tests red.

